sn47.31:4.2 | S: ‘This observation of an aspect of principles should be developed.’ … | T: “‘That contemplation of phenomena in phenomena is to be developed’ …
sn47.31:4.3 | S: ‘This observation of an aspect of principles has been developed.’ Such was the vision, knowledge, wisdom, realization, and light that arose in me regarding teachings not learned before from another.” | T: ‘That contemplation of phenomena in phenomena has been developed’—thus, bhikkhus, in regard to things unheard before, there arose in me vision, knowledge, wisdom, true knowledge, and light.”
sn47.31:4.4 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn56.14
sn56.14:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these four noble truths. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these Four Noble Truths.
sn56.14:1.2 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn56.14:1.3 | S: The noble truths of suffering, the origin of suffering, the cessation of suffering, and the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering. | T: The noble truth of suffering, the noble truth of the origin of suffering, the noble truth of the cessation of suffering, the noble truth of the way leading to the cessation of suffering.
sn56.14:2.1 | S: And what is the noble truth of suffering? | T: “And what, bhikkhus, is the noble truth of suffering?
sn56.14:2.2 | S: You should say: ‘The six interior sense fields’. | T: It should be said: the six internal sense bases.
sn56.14:2.3 | S: What six? | T: What six?
sn56.14:2.4 | S: The sense fields of the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body, and mind. | T: The eye base … the mind base.
sn56.14:2.5 | S: This is called the noble truth of suffering. | T: This is called the noble truth of suffering.”
sn56.14:3.1 | S: And what is the noble truth of the origin of suffering? … | T: (none)
sn56.14:3.2 | P: Yāyaṁ taṇhā ponobbhavikā nandirāgasahagatā tatratatrābhinandinī, seyyathidaṁ—kāmataṇhā, bhavataṇhā, vibhavataṇhā— | T: (none)
sn56.14:3.3 | P: idaṁ vuccati, bhikkhave, dukkhasamudayaṁ ariyasaccaṁ. | T: (none)
sn56.14:4.1 | S: the cessation of suffering? … | T: (none)
sn56.14:4.2 | P: Yo tassāyeva taṇhāya asesavirāganirodho cāgo paṭinissaggo mutti anālayo— | T: (none)
sn56.14:4.3 | P: idaṁ vuccati, bhikkhave, dukkhanirodhaṁ ariyasaccaṁ. | T: (none)
sn56.14:5.1 | S: the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering? | T: (none)
sn56.14:5.2 | S: It is simply this noble eightfold path … | T: (none)
sn56.14:5.3 | P: sammādiṭṭhi …pe… sammāsamādhi— | T: (none)
sn56.14:5.4 | P: idaṁ vuccati, bhikkhave, dukkhanirodhagāminī paṭipadā ariyasaccaṁ. | T: (none)
sn56.14:5.5 | P: Imāni kho, bhikkhave, cattāri ariyasaccāni. | T: (none)
sn56.14:6.1 | S: That’s why you should practice meditation …” | T: The rest of the sutta is identical with §13.
sn56.14:6.2 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an3.59
an3.59:1.1 | S: Then the brahmin Jānussoṇi went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. Seated to one side he said to the Buddha: | T: Then the brahmin Jāṇussoṇī approached the Blessed One … and said to him:
an3.59:1.2 | S: “Worthy Gotama, whoever has a sacrifice, an offering of food for ancestors, a dish of milk-rice prepared for an auspicious ceremony, or a gift to give, should give it to the brahmins who have mastered the three Vedic knowledges.” | T: “Master Gotama, whoever has a sacrifice, a memorial meal, an offering dish, or something to be given should give the gift to brahmins who are masters of the threefold knowledge.”
an3.59:1.3 | S: “But brahmin, how do the brahmins describe a brahmin who is proficient in the three Vedic knowledges?” | T: The Blessed One said: “But how, brahmin, do the brahmins describe a brahmin who is a master of the threefold knowledge?”
an3.59:1.4 | S: “Worthy Gotama, it’s when a brahmin is well born on both his mother’s and father’s side, stemming from a clean vessel, with irrefutable and impeccable genealogy back to the seventh paternal generation. He recites and remembers the hymns, and has mastered the three Vedas, together with their vocabularies and ritual performance, their phonology and word classification, and the testaments as fifth. He knows them word-by-word, and their grammar. He is well versed in cosmology and the marks of a great man. | T: “Here, Master Gotama, a brahmin is well born on both sides … as in AN3.58 … and skilled in the marks of a great man.
an3.59:1.5 | S: That’s how the brahmins describe a brahmin who is proficient in the three Vedic knowledges.” | T: It is in this way that the brahmins describe a master of the threefold knowledge.”
an3.59:2.1 | S: “Brahmin, a master of three knowledges according to the brahmins is quite different from a master of the three knowledges in the training of the Noble One.” | T: “Brahmin, a master of the threefold knowledge in the Noble One’s discipline is quite different from the one that the brahmins describe.”
an3.59:2.2 | S: “But worthy Gotama, how is one a master of the three knowledges in the training of the Noble One? | T: “But in what way, Master Gotama, is one a master of the threefold knowledge in the Noble One’s discipline?
an3.59:2.3 | S: Worthy Gotama, please teach me this.” | T: It would be good if Master Gotama would teach me the Dhamma in such a way as to make clear how one is a master of the threefold knowledge in the Noble One’s discipline.”
an3.59:2.4 | S: “Well then, brahmin, listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: “Well then, brahmin, listen and attend closely. I will speak.”
an3.59:2.5 | S: “Yes, worthy sir,” Jānussoṇi replied. | T: “Yes, sir,” the brahmin Jāṇussoṇī replied.
an3.59:2.6 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said this:
an3.59:3.1 | S: “Brahmin, it’s when a mendicant, quite secluded from sensual pleasures … | T: “Here, brahmin, secluded from sensual pleasures …
an3.59:3.2 | S: enters and remains in the fourth absorption. | T: all as in AN3.58, down to: …
an3.59:4.1 | S: When their mind has become immersed in samādhi like this—purified, bright, flawless, rid of corruptions, pliable, workable, steady, and imperturbable—they extend it toward recollection of past lives. | T: (none)
an3.59:4.2 | S: They recollect many kinds of past lives, with features and details. | T: (none)
an3.59:4.3 | S: This is the first knowledge that they attain. | T: (none)
an3.59:4.4 | S: Ignorance is banished and knowledge has arisen; darkness is banished and light has arisen, as happens for a meditator who is diligent, keen, and resolute. | T: (none)
an3.59:5.1 | S: When their mind has become immersed in samādhi like this—purified, bright, flawless, rid of corruptions, pliable, workable, steady, and imperturbable—they extend it toward knowledge of the death and rebirth of sentient beings. | T: (none)
an3.59:5.2 | S: With clairvoyance that is purified and surpasses the human, they understand how sentient beings pass on according to their deeds. | T: (none)
an3.59:5.3 | S: This is the second knowledge that they attain. | T: (none)
an3.59:5.4 | S: Ignorance is banished and knowledge has arisen; darkness is banished and light has arisen, as happens for a meditator who is diligent, keen, and resolute. | T: (none)
an3.59:6.1 | S: When their mind has become immersed in samādhi like this—purified, bright, flawless, rid of corruptions, pliable, workable, steady, and imperturbable—they extend it toward knowledge of the ending of defilements. | T: (none)
an3.59:6.2 | S: They truly understand: ‘This is suffering’ … ‘This is the origin of suffering’ … ‘This is the cessation of suffering’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of suffering’. | T: (none)
an3.59:6.3 | S: They truly understand: ‘These are defilements’ … ‘This is the origin of defilements’ … ‘This is the cessation of defilements’ … ‘This is the practice that leads to the cessation of defilements’. | T: (none)
an3.59:6.4 | S: Knowing and seeing like this, their mind is freed from the defilements of sensuality, desire to be reborn, and ignorance. | T: (none)
an3.59:6.5 | S: When they’re freed, they know they’re freed. | T: (none)
an3.59:6.6 | S: They understand: ‘Rebirth is ended, the spiritual journey has been completed, what had to be done has been done, there is nothing further for this place.’ | T: (none)
an3.59:6.7 | S: This is the third knowledge that they attain. | T: This is the third true knowledge attained by him.
an3.59:6.8 | S: Ignorance is banished and knowledge has arisen; darkness is banished and light has arisen, as happens for a meditator who is diligent, keen, and resolute. | T: Ignorance is dispelled, true knowledge has arisen; darkness is dispelled, light has arisen, as happens when one dwells heedful, ardent, and resolute.
an3.59:7.1 | S: One who is perfect in precepts and observances, | T: “One consummate in virtue and observances,
an3.59:7.2 | S: resolute and serene, | T: who is resolute and composed,
an3.59:7.3 | S: whose mind is mastered, | T: whose mind has been mastered,
an3.59:7.4 | S: unified, serene; | T: one-pointed and well concentrated;
an3.59:8.1 | S: who knows their past lives, | T: “one who knows his past abodes,
an3.59:8.2 | S: sees heaven and places of loss, | T: who sees heaven and the plane of misery,
an3.59:8.3 | S: and has attained the end of rebirth, | T: and has reached the destruction of birth
an3.59:8.4 | S: such a sage has perfect insight. | T: is a sage consummate in direct knowledge.
an3.59:9.1 | S: Because of these three knowledges | T: “Through these three kinds of knowledge
an3.59:9.2 | S: a brahmin is a master of the three knowledges. | T: one is a triple-knowledge brahmin.
an3.59:9.3 | S: That’s who I call a three-knowledge master, | T: I call him a triple-knowledge master,
an3.59:9.4 | S: and not the other <j>who repeats what they are told. | T: not the other who utters incantations.
an3.59:10.1 | S: This, brahmin, is a master of the three knowledges in the training of the Noble One.” | T: “It is in this way, brahmin, that one is a master of the threefold knowledge in the Noble One’s discipline.”
an3.59:10.2 | S: “Worthy Gotama, the master of three knowledges according to the brahmins is quite different from a master of the three knowledges in the training of the Noble One. | T: “A master of the threefold knowledge in the Noble One’s discipline, Master Gotama, is quite different from a master of the threefold knowledge according to the brahmins.
an3.59:10.3 | S: And, worthy Gotama, a master of three knowledges according to the brahmins is not worth a sixteenth part of a master of the three knowledges in the training of the Noble One. | T: And a master of the threefold knowledge according to the brahmins is not worth a sixteenth part of a master of the threefold knowledge in the Noble One’s discipline.
an3.59:11.1 | S: Excellent, worthy Gotama! Excellent! … | T: “Excellent, Master Gotama! …
an3.59:11.2 | S: From this day forth, may the worthy Gotama remember me as a lay follower who has gone for refuge for life.” | T: Let Master Gotama consider me a lay follower who from today has gone for refuge for life.”
an3.59:11.3 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.97
sn35.97:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you who lives negligently and who lives diligently. | T: “Bhikkhus, I will teach you about one who dwells negligently, and about one who dwells diligently.
sn35.97:1.2 | S: Listen … | T: Listen to that….
sn35.97:1.3 | S: And how does someone live negligently? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, does one dwell negligently?
sn35.97:1.4 | S: When you live with the eye faculty unrestrained, your mind becomes polluted when it comes to sights known by the eye. | T: If one dwells without restraint over the eye faculty, the mind is soiled among forms cognizable by the eye.
sn35.97:1.5 | S: When the mind is polluted, there’s no joy. | T: If the mind is soiled, there is no gladness.
sn35.97:1.6 | S: When there’s no joy, there’s no rapture. | T: When there is no gladness, there is no rapture.
sn35.97:1.7 | S: When there’s no rapture, there’s no tranquility. | T: When there is no rapture, there is no tranquillity.
sn35.97:1.8 | S: When there’s no tranquility, there’s suffering. | T: When there is no tranquillity, one dwells in suffering.
sn35.97:1.9 | S: When one is suffering, the mind does not become immersed in samādhi. | T: The mind of one who suffers does not become concentrated.
sn35.97:1.10 | S: When the mind is not immersed in samādhi, principles do not become clear. | T: When the mind is not concentrated, phenomena do not become manifest.
sn35.97:1.11 | S: Because principles have not become clear, you’re reckoned to live negligently. | T: Because phenomena do not become manifest, one is reckoned as ‘one who dwells negligently.’
sn35.97:1.12 | S: When you live with the ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: “If one dwells without restraint over the ear faculty, the mind is soiled among sounds cognizable by the ear….
sn35.97:1.13 | P: tassa byāsittacittassa …pe… | T: (none)
sn35.97:1.14 | P: pamādavihārītveva saṅkhaṁ gacchati …pe… | T: (none)
sn35.97:1.15 | S: mind faculty unrestrained, your mind becomes polluted when it comes to ideas known by the mind. | T: If one dwells without restraint over the mind faculty, the mind is soiled among mental phenomena cognizable by the mind….
sn35.97:1.16 | S: When the mind is polluted, there’s no joy. | T: (none)
sn35.97:1.17 | S: When there’s no joy, there’s no rapture. | T: (none)
sn35.97:1.18 | S: When there’s no rapture, there’s no tranquility. | T: (none)
sn35.97:1.19 | S: When there’s no tranquility, there’s suffering. | T: (none)
sn35.97:1.20 | S: When one is suffering, the mind does not become immersed in samādhi. | T: (none)
sn35.97:1.21 | S: When the mind is not immersed in samādhi, principles do not become clear. | T: (none)
sn35.97:1.22 | S: Because principles have not become clear, you’re reckoned to live negligently. | T: Because phenomena do not become manifest, one is reckoned as ‘one who dwells negligently.’
sn35.97:1.23 | S: That’s how someone lives negligently. | T: “It is in such a way, bhikkhus, that one dwells negligently.
sn35.97:2.1 | S: And how does someone live diligently? | T: “And how, bhikkhus, does one dwell diligently?
sn35.97:2.2 | S: When you live with the eye faculty restrained, your mind doesn’t become polluted when it comes to sights known by the eye. | T: If one dwells with restraint over the eye faculty, the mind is not soiled among forms cognizable by the eye.
sn35.97:2.3 | S: When the mind isn’t polluted, joy springs up. | T: If the mind is not soiled, gladness is born.
sn35.97:2.4 | S: Being joyful, rapture springs up. | T: When one is gladdened, rapture is born.
sn35.97:2.5 | S: When the mind is full of rapture, the body becomes tranquil. | T: When the mind is uplifted by rapture, the body becomes tranquil.
sn35.97:2.6 | S: When the body is tranquil, one feels bliss. | T: One tranquil in body experiences happiness.
sn35.97:2.7 | S: And when blissful, the mind becomes immersed in samādhi. | T: The mind of one who is happy becomes concentrated.
sn35.97:2.8 | S: When the mind is immersed in samādhi, principles become clear. | T: When the mind is concentrated, phenomena become manifest.
sn35.97:2.9 | S: Because principles have become clear, you’re reckoned to live diligently. | T: Because phenomena become manifest, one is reckoned as ‘one who dwells diligently.’
sn35.97:2.10 | S: When you live with the ear … nose … tongue … body … | T: “If one dwells with restraint over the ear faculty, the mind is not soiled among sounds cognizable by the ear….
sn35.97:2.11 | P: appamādavihārītveva saṅkhaṁ gacchati. | T: (none)
sn35.97:2.12 | S: mind faculty restrained, your mind doesn’t become polluted when it comes to ideas known by the mind. | T: If one dwells with restraint over the mind faculty, the mind is not soiled among mental phenomena cognizable by the mind….
sn35.97:2.13 | S: When the mind isn’t polluted, joy springs up. | T: (none)
sn35.97:2.14 | S: Being joyful, rapture springs up. | T: (none)
sn35.97:2.15 | S: When the mind is full of rapture, the body becomes tranquil. | T: (none)
sn35.97:2.16 | S: When the body is tranquil, one feels bliss. | T: (none)
sn35.97:2.17 | S: And when blissful, the mind becomes immersed in samādhi. | T: (none)
sn35.97:2.18 | S: When the mind is immersed in samādhi, principles become clear. | T: (none)
sn35.97:2.19 | S: Because principles have become clear, you’re reckoned to live diligently. | T: Because phenomena become manifest, one is reckoned as ‘one who dwells diligently.’
sn35.97:2.20 | S: That’s how someone lives diligently.” | T: “It is in such a way, bhikkhus, that one dwells diligently.”
sn35.97:2.21 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn36.24
sn36.24:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, before my awakening—when I was still unawakened but intent on awakening—I thought: | T: “Bhikkhus, before my enlightenment, while I was still a bodhisatta, not yet fully enlightened, it occurred to me:
sn36.24:1.2 | S: ‘What is feeling? What’s the origin of feeling? What’s the practice that leads to the origin of feeling? What’s the cessation of feeling? What’s the practice that leads to the cessation of feeling? | T: “What now is feeling? What is the origin of feeling? What is the way leading to the origination of feeling? What is the cessation of feeling? What is the way leading to the cessation of feeling?
sn36.24:1.3 | S: And what is feeling’s gratification, drawback, and escape?’ | T: What is the gratification in feeling? What is the danger? What is the escape?”
sn36.24:1.4 | S: Then it occurred to me: | T: “Then, bhikkhus, it occurred to me:
sn36.24:1.5 | S: ‘There are these three feelings: | T: ‘There are these three feelings …
sn36.24:1.6 | S: pleasant, painful, and neutral. | T: (none)
sn36.24:1.7 | S: These are called feelings. | T: (none)
sn36.24:1.8 | S: Feeling originates from contact. | T: (none)
sn36.24:1.9 | S: Craving is the practice that leads to the origin of feeling … | T: all as in preceding sutta …
sn36.24:1.10 | S: Removing and giving up desire and greed for feeling: this is its escape.’” | T: this is the escape from feeling.’”
sn36.24:1.11 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn47.27
sn47.27:1.1 | S: The same setting. | T: The same setting.
sn47.27:1.2 | P: Ekamantaṁ nisinno kho āyasmā sāriputto āyasmantaṁ anuruddhaṁ etadavoca: | T: Sitting to one side, the Venerable Sāriputta said to the Venerable Anuruddha:
sn47.27:1.3 | S: “Reverend, they speak of the one called ‘an adept’. | T: “Friend Anuruddha, it is said, ‘One beyond training, one beyond training.’
sn47.27:1.4 | S: How is an adept defined?” | T: In what way, friend, is one beyond training?”
sn47.27:1.5 | S: “Reverends, an adept is someone who has completely developed the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: “It is, friend, because one has completely developed the four establishments of mindfulness that one is beyond training.
sn47.27:2.1 | S: What four? | T: What four?…
sn47.27:2.2 | S: It’s when a mendicant meditates by observing an aspect of the body—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: (none)
sn47.27:2.3 | S: They meditate observing an aspect of feelings … | T: (none)
sn47.27:2.4 | S: mind … | T: as above …
sn47.27:2.5 | S: principles—keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world. | T: (none)
sn47.27:2.6 | S: An adept is someone who has completely developed the four kinds of mindfulness meditation.” | T: It is because one has completely developed these four establishments of mindfulness that one is beyond training.”
sn47.27:2.7 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.49
sn12.49:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn12.49:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, a learned noble disciple doesn’t think: | T: “Bhikkhus, an instructed noble disciple does not think:
sn12.49:1.3 | P: ‘kiṁ nu kho— | T: (none)
sn12.49:1.4 | S: ‘When what exists, what is? Due to the arising of what, what arises? | T: ‘When what exists does what come to be? With the arising of what does what arise?
sn12.49:1.5 | S: When what exists do name and form come to be? When what exists do the six sense fields … contact … feeling … craving … grasping … continued existence … rebirth … old age and death come to be?’ | T: When what exists do volitional formations come to be? When what exists does consciousness come to be? When what exists does name-and-form come to be?… When what exists does aging-and-death come to be?’
sn12.49:2.1 | S: Rather, a learned noble disciple has only knowledge about this that is independent of others: | T: “Rather, bhikkhus, the instructed noble disciple has knowledge about this that is independent of others:
sn12.49:2.2 | S: ‘When this exists, this comes to be; due to the arising of this, this arises. | T: ‘When this exists, that comes to be; with the arising of this, that arises.
sn12.49:2.3 | S: When ignorance exists choices come to be. | T: When there is ignorance, volitional formations come to be.
sn12.49:2.4 | S: When choices exist consciousness comes to be. | T: When there are volitional formations, consciousness comes to be.
sn12.49:2.5 | S: When consciousness exists name and form come to be. | T: When there is consciousness, name-and-form comes to be….
sn12.49:2.6 | S: When name and form exist the six sense fields come to be. | T: (none)
sn12.49:2.7 | S: When the six sense fields exist contact comes to be. | T: (none)

