sn12.49:4.10 | S: They understand: | T: He understands thus:
sn12.49:4.11 | S: ‘That is how this world ceases.’ | T: ‘In such a way the world ceases.’
sn12.49:5.1 | S: A noble disciple comes to truly understand the origin and disappearance of the world. Such a noble disciple is one who is called ‘one accomplished in view’, ‘one accomplished in vision’, ‘one who has come to the true teaching’, ‘one who sees this true teaching’, ‘one endowed with a trainee’s insight’, ‘one endowed with a trainee’s knowledge’, ‘one who has entered the stream of the teaching’, ‘a noble one with penetrative wisdom’, and also ‘one who stands knocking at the door to freedom from death’.” | T: “Bhikkhus, when a noble disciple thus understands as they really are the origin and the passing away of the world, he is then called a noble disciple who is accomplished in view, accomplished in vision, who has arrived at this true Dhamma, who sees this true Dhamma, who possesses a trainee’s knowledge, a trainee’s true knowledge, who has entered the stream of the Dhamma, a noble one with penetrative wisdom, one who stands squarely before the door to the Deathless.”
sn12.49:5.2 | P: amatadvāraṁ āhacca tiṭṭhati itipī”ti. | T: (none)
sn12.49:5.3 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn12.64
sn12.64:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: At Savatthī.
sn12.64:1.2 | S: “Mendicants, these four fuels maintain sentient beings that have been born and help those about to be born. | T: “Bhikkhus, there are these four kinds of nutriment for the maintenance of beings that have already come to be and for the assistance of those about to come to be.
sn12.64:1.3 | S: What four? | T: What four?
sn12.64:1.4 | S: Edible food, whether solid or subtle; contact is the second, mental intention the third, and consciousness the fourth. | T: The nutriment edible food, gross or subtle; second, contact; third, mental volition; fourth, consciousness.
sn12.64:1.5 | S: These are the four fuels that maintain sentient beings that have been born and help those about to be born. | T: These are the four kinds of nutriment for the maintenance of beings that have already come to be and for the assistance of those about to come to be.
sn12.64:2.1 | S: If there is desire, relishing, and craving for edible food, consciousness becomes established there and grows. | T: “If, bhikkhus, there is lust for the nutriment edible food, if there is delight, if there is craving, consciousness becomes established there and comes to growth.
sn12.64:2.2 | S: Where consciousness is established and grows, name and form are conceived. | T: Wherever consciousness becomes established and comes to growth, there is a descent of name-and-form.
sn12.64:2.3 | S: Where name and form are conceived, there is the growth of choices. | T: Where there is a descent of name-and-form, there is the growth of volitional formations.
sn12.64:2.4 | S: Where choices grow, there is regeneration into a new state of existence in the future. | T: Where there is the growth of volitional formations, there is the production of future renewed existence.
sn12.64:2.5 | S: Where there is regeneration into a new state of existence in the future, there is rebirth, old age, and death in the future. | T: Where there is the production of future renewed existence, there is future birth, aging, and death.
sn12.64:2.6 | S: Where there is rebirth, old age, and death in the future, I say this is full of sorrow, anguish, and distress. | T: Where there is future birth, aging, and death, I say that is accompanied by sorrow, anguish, and despair.
