sn12.64:7.8 | S: Where there is no rebirth, old age, and death in the future, I say there’s no sorrow, anguish, and distress. | T: Where there is no future birth, aging, and death, I say that is without sorrow, anguish, and despair.
sn12.64:8.1 | S: Suppose there was a bungalow or a hall with a peaked roof, with windows on the northern, southern, or eastern side. When the sun rises and a ray of light enters through a window, where would it land?” | T: “Suppose, bhikkhus, there was a house or a hall with a peaked roof, with windows on the northern, southern, and eastern sides. When the sun rises and a beam of light enters through a window, where would it become established?”
sn12.64:8.2 | S: “On the western wall, sir.” | T: “On the western wall, venerable sir.”
sn12.64:8.3 | S: “If there was no western wall, where would it land?” | T: “If there were no western wall, where would it become established?”
sn12.64:8.4 | S: “On the ground, sir.” | T: “On the earth, venerable sir.”
sn12.64:8.5 | S: “If there was no ground, where would it land?” | T: “If there were no earth, where would it become established?”
sn12.64:8.6 | S: “In water, sir.” | T: “On the water, venerable sir.”
sn12.64:8.7 | S: “If there was no water, where would it land?” | T: “If there were no water, where would it become established?”
sn12.64:8.8 | S: “It wouldn’t land, sir.” | T: “It would not become established anywhere, venerable sir.”
sn12.64:8.9 | S: “In the same way, if there is no desire, relishing, and craving for edible food, consciousness does not become established there and doesn’t grow. … | T: “So too, bhikkhus, if there is no lust for the nutriment edible food …
